  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nSurface high pressure building overhead today will linger into\nWednesday, delivering dry and quiet weather across the Central\nAppalachians and Middle Ohio Valley. With clear skies, light to\ncalm surface winds, and small temperature and dewpoint\ndepressions, radiational cooling has promoted dense river \nvalley fog early this morning. This fog will dissipate rapidly \nafter sunrise this morning. Afternoon highs will range from the \nupper 70s to lower 80s across the lowlands and the mid to upper \n70s in the mountains under clear to partly cloudy skies. \n\nIdeal radiational cooling conditions will recur tonight into\nearly Wednesday morning, supporting another round of dense river\nvalley fog. Surface high pressure shifts east during Wednesday,\ndeveloping weak southerly return flow. Daytime highs Wednesday \nwill moderate into the mid 80s in the Metro Valley and lowlands \nwith comfortable humidity levels persisting through the day. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA prefrontal trough approaches from the northwest late Wednesday\nnight into Thursday morning. Shower and thunderstorm activity\nassociated with this trough is expected to be in a waning state\nas it moves into central OH with loss of diurnal heating.\n\nLow-level moisture pooling ahead of a cold front will push \nsurface dewpoints into the upper 60s to lower 70s during the day\nThursday. Diurnal heating driving highs into the mid to upper \n80s will yield mixed-layer CAPE values of 1500 to 2500 J/kg \nacross the area. Deep-layer vertical shear of 30 to 35KTs will \nprovide sufficient kinematic support for convective \norganization, favoring multicell clusters and transient \nsupercell structures. Steep low-level lapse rates and a mid-\nlevel dry layer will generate DCAPE values of 1000 to 1300 J/kg,\nelevating the threat for localized wind gusts capable of tree \ndamage. Some marginally severe hail is also possible, although \nwet bulb zero heights of 12-13kft would suggest that this would \nlikely be limited to any transient supercellular structures. \nConvection will track southeast across the region Thursday \nafternoon and evening before exiting overnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nAn upper-level trough lingering along the East Coast on Friday\nwill push a secondary, reinforcing cold front across the area.\nGiven limited moisture return in the wake of Thursday frontal\npassage, this boundary will pass largely dry, though an isolated\nshower cannot be completely ruled out over the higher terrain of\nthe Northeast Mountains. \n\nHeading into the weekend and early next week, an upper-level\nridge amplifies across the central United States, placing the\nregion under a broad northwest flow regime aloft. Surface high\npressure building in from the Great Lakes will maintain dry\nweather and comfortable humidity on Friday and Saturday.\nTemperatures will trend warmer Sunday into early next week as\nthe surface high slides east and upper-level heights rise,\nreturning high temperatures to the upper 80s and lower 90s\nacross the lowlands.",
